::: {.doc-subject}

Add a windmill graph Wd(k, n) to the current graph.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L9646-L9777){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.add_windmill_graph(
    n,
    k,
    type=None,
    label=True,
    rel=None,
)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Windmill graph](../assets/graphs/add-windmill-graph.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="Three windmill graphs side by side, each a hub shared by several blades: three triangles (the friendship graph) for n=3 and k=3, four triangles for n=4, and three complete graphs on four nodes for k=4. Blades meet only at the hub, so removing it leaves n separate pieces." width="100%"}

Creates `n` complete graphs ("blades") of `k` nodes each that all share one hub node.
The graph has `n(k - 1) + 1` nodes and `n * k(k - 1)/2` edges. The hub is a cut node
joined to everything, and removing it leaves `n` separate cliques of `k - 1` nodes. With
`k=3` the result is the friendship graph (`n` triangles sharing a node), and with `k=2`
it is a star.

The hub is created first, then the other `k - 1` nodes of each blade in turn. In a
directed graph every edge points from the earlier-created node to the later one, so the
hub's edges all point outward. The graph is appended without touching or connecting to
existing nodes, and the whole construction is recorded as a single step.

Windmill graphs are useful for:

- modeling overlapping groups that share one member (a person who belongs to several
  otherwise separate teams)
- test cases for articulation points, biconnected components, and overlapping community
  detection, where the hub belongs to every group
- the friendship theorem: graphs in which every two nodes have exactly one common
  neighbor are exactly the friendship graphs
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[n]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Number of blades (cliques). Must be at least `2`.

<code>[k]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Number of nodes in each blade, counting the shared hub. Must be at least `2`.

<code>[type]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Optional value stored in the `type` attribute of every new node. If `None` (default),
    no `type` attribute is set.

<code>[label]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[True]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   If `True` (default), each new node is labelled with its node ID as a string; the hub
    gets the first number. A label that is already used raises `ValueError` before
    anything is added. If `False`, the nodes are left unlabelled.

<code>[rel]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Optional value stored in the `rel` attribute of every new edge. If `None` (default),
    no `rel` attribute is set.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Self for method chaining. The graph is modified in place.
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If `n=` or `k=` is less than `2`.
:::

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~add_star_graph`) 

:   Add a star graph.

[](`~add_full_graph`) 

:   Add a complete graph.

[](`~articulation_points`) 

:   Find the nodes whose removal disconnects the graph.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

A windmill graph is `n` complete graphs of `k` nodes that share one node, the hub. Three
triangles sharing a hub, the friendship graph, have `7` nodes and `9` edges:

::: {#cdb54fb4 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

graph = ec.Graph().add_windmill_graph(n=3, k=3)

graph.count_nodes(), graph.count_edges()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
(7, 9)
```
:::
:::


The hub is node `"1"`, and it's the only cut node: deleting it leaves three separate
edges:

::: {#e5627369 .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
hub = graph.articulation_points()
rest = graph.subgraph([n for n in graph.get_node_ids() if n not in hub])

graph.get_label_from_id(hub[0]), rest.count_weakly_connected_components()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
('1', 3)
```
:::
:::


### Triangles or cliques of four

`k=` sets the size of each blade. Three triangles on the left and three complete graphs of
four on the right, with the hub highlighted. The larger blades double the number of edges,
from `9` to `18`, and raise the hub's degree from `6` to `9`:

::: {#f0b5b524 .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
fours = ec.Graph().add_windmill_graph(n=3, k=4)

fours.count_nodes(), fours.count_edges(), fours.get_degree("1")
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}
```
(10, 18, 9)
```
:::
:::


